#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105813 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "969d32c6-b343-5e1f-9521-94ff346645b3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Correct Limits of a Given Function when Simplification is Required"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7e3d6fb3-96ca-598d-b899-4cfc00c51648" :question/math-academy-id "q-107412" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3x-2}{4-{x}^{2}},$ which of the following statements is correct?\n\nI. - ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty $\nII. - ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=\\infty $\nIII. - ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty $" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3x-2}{4-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+2)(2x-1)}{(x-2)(x+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is  $x\\ne \\pm 2,$ and we notice that the function can be simplified:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=-\\frac{2x-1}{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we see that the function has the only vertical asymptote $x=2.$ So statement II is false.\n\nTo find  ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x),$ we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=2$ from the left and right.\n\n| $x$ | $1.99$ | $1.999$ | $2$ | $2.001$ | $2.01$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $298$ | $2\\,998$ | $\\text{undefined}$ | $-3\\,002$ | $-302$ |\n\nAll values are positive and get larger and larger as $x\\to 2$ from the left and all values are negative and get smaller and smaller as $x\\to 2$ from the right . Therefore, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty \\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=\\infty \n$$\n\nConsequently, statement I is true and III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is correct. A plot of the function is shown below.\n\n![](images/9e/9e96d6a81813c1c89a498d0206234f2685f0a53c1bab626ce7430d84f9c0f506.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233198 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096633 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "050f0089-3abb-5b33-a522-9fcf6da51184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{1+\\frac{9k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247462} {:answer/id #uuid "0c7ba62d-655c-5d2f-9e39-d57cfcb4fd9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247536} {:answer/id #uuid "967f4525-a652-56db-858b-313b2768da07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{9+\\frac{4k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248762} {:answer/id #uuid "9e70dee7-9dc6-5645-af86-ccdb7c1fd656" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{1+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248830} {:answer/id #uuid "c19dabf5-82d5-51cb-a5a0-053c1d283106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c7ba62d-655c-5d2f-9e39-d57cfcb4fd9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247536} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed8515fc-083c-5484-a9e2-8b6570186b66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de045223-79af-5400-bb3f-2538c755ef59" :question/math-academy-id "q-113941" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{4}^{9}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x},$ the lower bound is $a=4$ and the upper bound is $b=9.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{9-4}{n}=\\frac{5}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(4+k\\cdot \\frac{5}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(4+\\frac{5k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064381 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dabb3127-172e-5a68-8e27-d22057f8ea8e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Related Rate With a Sphere: Word Problem"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2d45e40-2970-570b-ba18-c7ae81412efd" :question/math-academy-id "q-116735" :question/problem "![](images/63/63fe9cf3a61a5cef09f9c19e16a18318338faa6837eeb77b83f49b636157bb95.png)\n\nA sphere is decreasing in volume at a rate of $54\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ Calculate the rate at which the radius of the sphere is decreasing at the moment when the radius is $3\\,\\text{cm}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon and $V$ its volume. Both $r$ and $V$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-54\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=3\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the volume formula for the sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-54\\pi $ and $r=3,$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-54\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi (3)^{2} \\\\ -54\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 36\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the sphere is decreasing at a rate of $1.5\\text{ cm/s}$ when the radius is $3\\,\\text{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6005f9a-3eba-5f8d-909d-894d9cd24d12" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Expression"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ac66977-fb7e-55ea-b01a-306cc1fa6e11" :question/math-academy-id "q-138848" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}x}.$  The derivative of this function for $x\\in (0,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{'}=\\frac{g(x)\\operatorname{sin}x+h(x)\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x)+h(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}\\cdot \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+6x)^{1/2}\\operatorname{sin}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}y & =\\operatorname{ln}(({x}^{2}+6x)^{1/2}\\operatorname{sin}x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}y & =\\operatorname{ln}(({x}^{2}+6x)^{1/2})+\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{sin}x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{2}+6x)+\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{sin}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{2}+6x)+\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{sin}x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{'} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+6x}\\cdot (2x+6)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{'}}{y} & =\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\operatorname{cot}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =y(\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\operatorname{cot}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}x}(\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\operatorname{cot}x) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}x}(\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}x}(\\frac{(x+3)\\operatorname{sin}x+({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}x}{({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{sin}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}x|}{\\operatorname{sin}x}\\frac{(x+3)\\operatorname{sin}x+({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that $x\\in (0,\\pi ),$ then $|\\operatorname{sin}x|=\\operatorname{sin}x,$ so the fraction $\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}x|}{\\operatorname{sin}x}$ simplifies to $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =\\frac{(x+3)\\operatorname{sin}x+({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x)+h(x)=x+3+{x}^{2}+6x={x}^{2}+7x+3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233510 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184240 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a58f115-f368-5171-af73-37084085b42a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit Involving Trigonometric Functions and Their Inverses"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f20a6552-65e3-58b7-b9da-e6bf558e0b66" :question/math-academy-id "q-152889" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\,1/2}\\operatorname{tan}(6\\operatorname{arccos}(x))=$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$ \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1/2}\\operatorname{tan}(6\\operatorname{arccos}(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}(6\\operatorname{arccos}(\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}(6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}(2\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "583e8820-a8c9-5c94-84cc-d5ff1ee239b4" :knowledge-point/title "Removing Jump Discontinuities From Rational Functions"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d3161c6d-15b1-5502-a086-55781c56e488" :question/math-academy-id "q-38592" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the function defined below. For what value of $k$ is $f(x)$ continuous at $x=-1?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{x-1}, & x<-1 \\\\ 2x-k, & x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the definition of $f(x)$, the left-sided limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}\\frac{k}{x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{-1-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}(2x-k) \\\\  & =2(-1)-k \\\\  & =-2-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also note that $f(-1)=-2-k.$ \n\nSo, for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ -2-k & =-\\frac{k}{2} \\\\ -4-2k & =-k \\\\ k & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=-4$ removes the discontinuity at $x=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078407 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f5c089a-9506-5a0e-83ae-48efa8fd314c" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Derivatives in Limits Similar to the Instantaneous Rate of Change Formula"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ef423eeb-a6a9-5a03-bbd4-f26b7d9971da" :question/math-academy-id "q-4740" :question/problem "${lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2+h}-{e}^{2}}{h}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nL={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2+h}-{e}^{2}}{h}.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(a)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h}.\n$$\n\nWe see that if we let $f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $a=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(2) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(2+h)-f(2)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2+h}-{e}^{2}}{h}=L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis confirms that $L={f}^{'}(2).$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we have\n \n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{f}^{'}(2)={e}^{2},\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude tha $L={e}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234079 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078608 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf9fa7f8-708a-5006-9b9b-ea2c0334cb2c" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Central Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa4f2b60-2a66-5f4f-a213-69e38a19fcc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-47501" :question/problem "Anna decides to go jogging. Her position from the starting point, measured in meters, is given by the function $s(t),$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. The values of $s(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| **$t$** | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ | $11$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $s(t)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $14$ | $25$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nApproximate the velocity ${s}^{'}(t)$ at the point $t=7$ using a central difference approximation with the step size $h=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{s}^{'}(t)\\approx \\frac{s(t+h)-s(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=7$ with step size $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{'}(7) & \\approx \\frac{s(7+2)-s(7-2)}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{s(9)-s(5)}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{14-4}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{4} \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the velocity of Anna at the instant of time $t=7$ is $2.5\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234323 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100812 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98e43a1e-2073-593b-9b55-4f9c25c356a8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Limit of a Function Given as a Table of Values Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fed62691-6668-5c74-90ca-07c937644bed" :question/math-academy-id "q-72361" :question/problem "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are twice-differentiable on an open interval containing $x=1.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are given below:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{'}(x)$ | ${f}^{''}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{'}(x)$ | ${g}^{''}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=1$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x} & ={\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(1)+1}{g(1)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(f(x)+x)^{'}}{(g(x)-x)^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{'}(x)+1}{{g}^{'}(x)-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=1$ again, but once more, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{'}(x)+1}{{g}^{'}(x)-1} & ={\\frac{{f}^{'}(x)+1}{{g}^{'}(x)-1}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{f}^{'}(1)+1}{{g}^{'}(1)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply L'Hopital's rule again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{'}(x)+1}{{g}^{'}(x)-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{({f}^{'}(x)+1)^{'}}{({g}^{'}(x)-1)^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{''}(x)}{{g}^{''}(x)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this time, when we substitute $x=1,$ we are actually able to determine the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{''}(x)}{{g}^{''}(x)} & ={\\frac{{f}^{''}(x)}{{g}^{''}(x)}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{f}^{''}(1)}{{g}^{''}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{-2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+x}{g(x)-x}=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234351 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129091 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea5bdd3b-810c-56e6-acfd-4de0e0cfc4e8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of Rational Functions With Common Factors"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3cc6eed-e52f-5a0b-bc51-e9a47cb4eee9" :question/math-academy-id "q-73427" :question/problem "Find all of the points where the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{2}+4x+3}$ has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{2}+4x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(x+3)}{(x+1)(x+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cancel{(x+3)}}{(x+1)\\cancel{(x+3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx+1=0\\,⟹\\,x=-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-1$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234352 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bf7b7ef5-50f5-5f18-b878-7ff1f9484b60" :question/math-academy-id "q-73492" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}x, & x\\le 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}x, & x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nI. - $[-2,-1]$\nII. - $[-1,1]$\nIII. - $(0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[-2,-1].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}x$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,-1].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[-1,1].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(\\operatorname{ln}x)=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we note that the interval $(0,1]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}]}
